# Research Misconduct Policy

**{{ org_name }}** — a 501(c)(3) nonprofit research institute incorporated in {{ state }}
EIN: {{ ein }}

## 1. Purpose

{{ org_name }} ("the Organization") is committed to the highest standards of integrity in all research activities it conducts, sponsors, or supports. This policy establishes procedures for identifying, reporting, and addressing research misconduct in compliance with the federal regulations applicable to institutions that receive or may receive funding from the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services (42 CFR Part 93) and the National Science Foundation (45 CFR 689). These procedures apply regardless of the current funding source of the research in question.

## 2. Scope

This policy applies to all directors, officers, employees, fellows, trainees, volunteers, contractors, and collaborators who propose, conduct, report, or review research on behalf of or in association with the Organization (collectively, "Covered Persons"). It covers all research activities including, but not limited to, proposals, laboratory and field work, data collection and analysis, publications, and grant applications.

## 3. Definitions

**Research misconduct** means fabrication, falsification, or plagiarism (FFP) in proposing, performing, or reviewing research, or in reporting research results.

- **Fabrication** — making up data or results and recording or reporting them.
- **Falsification** — manipulating research materials, equipment, or processes, or changing or omitting data or results such that the research is not accurately represented in the research record.
- **Plagiarism** — the appropriation of another person's ideas, processes, results, or words without giving appropriate credit.

Research misconduct **does not include** honest error or differences of opinion. A finding of research misconduct requires that:

1. There be a significant departure from accepted practices of the relevant research community;
2. The misconduct be committed intentionally, knowingly, or recklessly; and
3. The allegation be proven by a preponderance of the evidence.

**Allegation** — a disclosure of possible research misconduct through any means of communication to an institutional official.

**Complainant** — the person who in good faith makes an allegation of research misconduct.

**Respondent** — the person against whom an allegation of research misconduct is directed.

**Deciding Official** — the Board Chair, who has authority to receive allegations, oversee proceedings, and make final determinations on behalf of the Organization. If the Board Chair is the respondent, the Vice-Chair shall serve as Deciding Official.

**Research record** — the record of data or results that embody the facts resulting from research inquiry, including but not limited to proposals, laboratory records, progress reports, abstracts, theses, oral presentations, internal reports, journal articles, and any documents and materials provided to the federal sponsor.

## 4. Rights and Responsibilities

### The Organization

- Foster a research environment that promotes integrity and discourages misconduct.
- Respond promptly and fairly to all allegations.
- Protect the positions and reputations of good-faith complainants and cooperating witnesses.
- Protect the rights of respondents, including the presumption of good faith until a finding is made.
- Cooperate with and report to the Office of Research Integrity (ORI) at HHS or the Office of Inspector General (OIG) at NSF as required by law.

### Complainants

- Report suspected misconduct in good faith and cooperate with proceedings.
- Complainants are entitled to protection from retaliation under the Organization's Whistleblower Protection Policy and under 42 CFR 93.300(d).

### Respondents

- Have the right to be informed of allegations against them (at the investigation phase or earlier if needed to preserve evidence).
- Have the right to review evidence, present a response, and be accompanied by an advisor (including legal counsel) during interviews.
- Must cooperate with proceedings and preserve all research records related to the allegation.

## 5. Receiving Allegations

Any person may submit an allegation of research misconduct to the Deciding Official (Board Chair) in writing. Allegations may also be made verbally, in which case the Deciding Official shall promptly reduce them to writing. Anonymous allegations will be accepted and evaluated, though anonymity may limit the ability to conduct a thorough review.

Upon receipt of an allegation, the Deciding Official shall:

1. Acknowledge receipt within five (5) business days (unless anonymous).
2. Immediately assess whether the allegation falls within the scope of this policy (i.e., whether it involves FFP in research).
3. Take immediate steps to secure and preserve all relevant research records and evidence.

## 6. Assessment Phase

The Deciding Official shall conduct a preliminary assessment to determine whether the allegation is sufficiently credible and specific to warrant an inquiry. This assessment should be completed within fifteen (15) calendar days. If the allegation does not meet the definition of research misconduct, the Deciding Official shall document the determination and notify the complainant that no further action will be taken under this policy. The matter may be referred to other institutional processes if appropriate.

## 7. Inquiry Phase

If the assessment determines that the allegation is credible and within scope, the Deciding Official shall initiate an inquiry — a preliminary fact-finding to determine whether a full investigation is warranted.

**Timeline**: The inquiry must be completed within sixty (60) calendar days of initiation.

**Process**:

1. The Deciding Official may conduct the inquiry personally or appoint an Inquiry Committee of one or more qualified persons. For a small institution, the Deciding Official may engage an external investigator or subject-matter expert to assist with or conduct the inquiry.
2. The inquiry shall review the evidence and interview the complainant, respondent, and any key witnesses.
3. The respondent shall be notified and given the opportunity to provide a written response.

**Report**: At the conclusion of the inquiry, the Deciding Official (or Inquiry Committee) shall prepare a written report that includes:

- The name and position of the respondent;
- A description of the allegation;
- A summary of the evidence reviewed;
- A determination of whether an investigation is warranted; and
- The basis for the determination.

The respondent shall receive a copy of the inquiry report and have ten (10) calendar days to submit written comments, which shall be appended to the report.

If the inquiry determines that an investigation **is not** warranted, the Deciding Official shall document the finding, notify the complainant and respondent, and close the matter. If the inquiry determines that an investigation **is** warranted, the Deciding Official shall notify the respondent, secure all evidence, and initiate the investigation phase. If the research is federally funded, the Organization shall notify ORI (HHS) or OIG (NSF) on or before the date the investigation begins.

## 8. Investigation Phase

The investigation is a formal examination to determine whether research misconduct occurred, and if so, its extent and severity.

**Timeline**: The investigation must be completed within one hundred twenty (120) calendar days of initiation. If an extension is necessary, the Deciding Official shall document the reasons and, for federally funded research, request approval from ORI or OIG.

**Process**:

1. The Deciding Official shall appoint an Investigation Committee or engage an external investigator with appropriate expertise and no conflict of interest with either the complainant or respondent.
2. The investigation shall include examination of all relevant research records and evidence, and interviews with the complainant, respondent, and witnesses.
3. The respondent shall be given the opportunity to be interviewed, to present evidence, and to be accompanied by an advisor during any interview.

**Burden of proof**: The Organization bears the burden of proving misconduct by a **preponderance of the evidence** (i.e., it is more likely than not that misconduct occurred).

**Report**: The Investigation Committee (or external investigator) shall prepare a written report that includes:

- A description of the allegation and the specific research misconduct considered;
- A description of the evidence and sources consulted;
- Findings of fact based on the evidence;
- A determination for each allegation: whether research misconduct occurred, the type and severity, and whether it was committed intentionally, knowingly, or recklessly;
- Identification of any federally funded research affected; and
- Recommended sanctions or corrective actions.

The respondent shall receive a copy of the draft investigation report and have thirty (30) calendar days to submit written comments, which shall be appended to the final report.

## 9. Interim Protections

Throughout all phases of the proceedings, the Organization shall:

- **Protect the complainant**: Guard against retaliation, consistent with the Whistleblower Protection Policy.
- **Preserve evidence**: Take all reasonable steps to secure research records, data, and materials at the time the allegation is received, and maintain their integrity throughout the process.
- **Protect the respondent**: Take reasonable steps to protect the respondent's reputation and position until a final determination is made. The respondent shall be presumed innocent unless and until misconduct is established.
- **Protect federal interests**: If at any time during the inquiry or investigation the Deciding Official determines that (a) public health or safety is at risk, (b) federal resources or interests need protection, (c) research activities should be suspended, or (d) there is a reasonable indication of a violation of civil or criminal law, the Deciding Official shall immediately notify ORI (HHS) or OIG (NSF) and any other relevant authority.

## 10. Reporting to Federal Agencies

The Organization shall report to ORI or OIG as required by 42 CFR Part 93 and 45 CFR 689, including:

- Notification when an investigation is opened (on or before the start date);
- Interim reports as required by the agency;
- The final investigation report and institutional determination;
- Notification of any special circumstances requiring immediate reporting (Section 9 above).

For research not funded by HHS or NSF, the Organization shall report to the relevant federal agency in accordance with its applicable regulations.

## 11. Institutional Determination and Sanctions

Based on the investigation report, the Deciding Official shall make a final determination and present it to the Board of Directors.

If research misconduct is found, the Board may impose sanctions or corrective actions proportionate to the severity and nature of the misconduct, including but not limited to:

- A letter of reprimand;
- Required training or supervision;
- Removal from a project or denial of future research privileges;
- Correction, retraction, or withdrawal of affected publications or grant applications;
- Restitution of misused funds;
- Termination of employment or contractual relationship;
- Removal from the Board of Directors;
- Referral to law enforcement if criminal conduct is suspected.

## 12. Appeals

The respondent may appeal the final determination in writing to the Board of Directors within thirty (30) calendar days of receiving the determination. The appeal must specify the grounds (e.g., procedural error, new evidence, disproportionate sanction). The Board shall review the appeal at its next regular meeting or a special meeting called for that purpose, and shall issue a written decision. The Board's decision on appeal is final at the institutional level.

For federally funded research, the respondent retains the right to contest findings through ORI's or OIG's own review and appeal processes under the applicable federal regulations.

## 13. Record Retention

The Organization shall retain all records related to research misconduct proceedings — including allegations, evidence, inquiry and investigation reports, respondent comments, institutional determinations, and appeal decisions — for a minimum of seven (7) years after completion of the proceeding, or for the duration required by the applicable federal agency, whichever is longer. Records shall be maintained securely and made available to ORI, OIG, or other authorized federal personnel upon request.

## 14. Small-Institution Considerations

As a small nonprofit research institute, the Organization may not have standing committees or in-house investigators with the requisite expertise. Accordingly:

- The Board Chair, serving as Deciding Official, may engage qualified external investigators, subject-matter experts, or legal counsel at any stage of these proceedings.
- Costs of external assistance shall be borne by the Organization as an operational expense.
- The Organization may consult with ORI or OIG for guidance on procedural matters at any time.

## 15. Amendments

This policy may be amended by a resolution of the Board of Directors at any duly convened meeting at which a quorum is present. Amendments shall be consistent with 42 CFR Part 93 and any other applicable federal regulations.

